//? Title: Nearest-neighbor traveling-salesman tours
//? Subtitle: Actual greedy scans, the original three cities and bounded optimal references
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Build a closed route visiting every city once before returning to the start.
Choosing the nearest unvisited city gives a quick feasible route but can make
expensive later choices. We inspect actual candidate decisions, retain the
original three-city example, compare a small geometric route with bounded
enumeration, and exhibit a directed family where greedy cost grows while the
optimum stays fixed.
)

\toc

> A route invariant, not an optimality proof

Start with one visited city. Each iteration scans city IDs in ascending order
and appends an unvisited city with minimum outgoing distance, retaining the
first on exact ties. Thus the partial route contains distinct selected cities,
and one new city is marked per iteration. After n−1 selections, every city has
appeared exactly once; appending the original start closes the route. Complete
finite nonnegative edge weights guarantee a feasible finite sequence, although a
produced cost sum can overflow and then throws.

This invariant proves route feasibility, not optimality. The API permits
asymmetric matrices and does not require the triangle inequality or a zero
diagonal. Its arbitrary-matrix domain is therefore broader than the symmetric
metric setting of many approximation guarantees. The explicit scan costs O(n
squared), plus the same-order complete input validation; driver/output storage
is O(n) excluding the supplied matrix.

> Retain the original three-city example

The original matrix has rows(0,1,2),(1,0,1),(2,1,0). From city0, the actual scan
chooses1 then2 and returns to0. Its edge weights1,1,2 sum to4. The diagram
unfolds the closed route into four positions so the return edge is visible
instead of hiding overlapping collinear geometric edges. The table reads actual
eligible-candidate tests.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/nearest_tsp_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto t =
        dm::algorithms::nearest_neighbor_tsp_trace({{0, 1, 2}, {1, 0, 1},
                                                    {2, 1, 0}});
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < t.analysis.result.tour.size(); ++i) {
        auto id = std::to_string(i);
        d.node(id, {5.0 * i, 0},
               std::format("Actual route position {}\ncity {}", i,
                           t.analysis.result.tour[i]));
        if (i)
            d.edge(std::to_string(i - 1), id,
                   std::format("cost {}", t.edges[i - 1].distance));
    }
    std::print("| actual current city | eligible candidate | distance | first eligible | best updated |\n| " "---: | ---: | ---: | --- | --- |\n");
    for (auto &r : t.trials)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", r.from, r.candidate,
                   r.distance, r.first, r.improved);
    std::print("\nOriginal actual route: {}, cost: {}.\n",
               t.analysis.result.tour, t.analysis.result.cost);
    d.save_svg("build/nearest_tsp_tiny.svg");
}
```
