> A directed nonmetric greedy trap

For rows(0,1,2),(1,0,M),(1,1,0), start0 chooses the one-unit edge to city1
before the two-unit edge to city2. It is then forced to pay M to reach2 and one
to return, costing M+2. The other anchored cycle0,2,1,0 costs4. For M at least2,
those are the only two anchored tours, so the optimum is4. The mathematical
ratio(M+2)/4 grows with M, showing why no constant approximation ratio follows
on arbitrary directed/nonmetric instances.

The finite native sweep below verifies prescribed integer-weight members of that
family. It does not transfer the counterexample to a symmetric Euclidean-metric
claim. The matrix generator and small exact comparator live in the library.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/nearest_tsp_adversary.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<double> x, ratio;
    std::print("| expensive directed edge M | native greedy cost | enumerated optimum | observed ratio |\n| " "---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (double m : {2., 5., 10., 100.}) {
        auto d = dm::algorithms::nearest_neighbor_tsp_adversary(m);
        auto g = dm::algorithms::nearest_neighbor_tsp(d);
        auto o = dm::algorithms::enumerate_tsp(d);
        x.push_back(m);
        ratio.push_back(g.cost / o.cost);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", m, g.cost, o.cost, g.cost / o.cost);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.points(x, ratio, "actual cost ratio", {220, 38, 38}, 6).title(
        "Directed nonmetric family: greedy cost versus optimum 4").x_label(
        "expensive directed edge M").y_label("greedy cost / enumerated cost").save_svg("build/nearest_tsp_adversary.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| expensive directed edge M | native greedy cost | enumerated optimum | observed ratio |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 2 | 4 | 4 | 1 |
| 5 | 7 | 4 | 1.75 |
| 10 | 12 | 4 | 3 |
| 100 | 102 | 4 | 25.5 |
\image(figures/nearest_tsp_adversary.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual ratios for a hand-derived directed greedy-trap family.)
\alttext(The optimum stays4 while the observed greedy cost is M+2 for the tested integer parameters.)

> Starting cities and actual work

On the six-city geometric fixture, every start is tried separately through the
high-level native API. The table preserves its route and actual selected work.
Candidate checks include all n city IDs at each of n−1 selections, totaling
n(n−1). Only eligible candidates after the first require a strict distance
comparison, totaling(n−1)(n−2)/2. Exactly n route edges are summed after closing
the nonempty route. These counts exclude allocation, validation and general CPU
costs.

Matrix shape and every finite nonnegative edge are validated before
route/capture work. A nonempty start must be valid. Empty input returns
zero/empty and ignores start; a singleton repeats its start and uses its
supplied diagonal cost. Greedy sum overflow throws. The bounded enumerator
saturates overflowing unused candidate tours to infinity, allowing a finite
minimum to survive; it throws if all candidates overflow. Its default ten-vertex
cap is checked before enumeration, and zero disables it.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/nearest_tsp_starts.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto d =
        dm::algorithms::euclidean_tsp_distances(dm::algorithms::nearest_neighbor_tsp_demo_points());
    std::vector<double> costs;
    std::vector<std::string> labels;
    std::print("| start | actual route | rounded cost | visited-ID tests | distance comparisons | edges " "summed |\n| ---: | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t s = 0; s < d.size(); ++s) {
        auto a = dm::algorithms::nearest_neighbor_tsp_analyze(d, s);
        costs.push_back(a.result.cost);
        labels.push_back(std::to_string(s));
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", s, a.result.tour,
                   a.result.cost, a.candidate_checks, a.distance_comparisons,
                   a.edges_summed);
    }
    auto reference = dm::algorithms::enumerate_tsp(d);
    auto p =
        dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(costs, labels, "greedy cost", {22, 163, 74});
    p.abline_h(reference.cost, {220, 38, 38}, 1).legend({"greedy cost",
                                                         "enumerated start0"},
                                                        {{22, 163, 74},
                                                         {220, 38, 38}}).title(
        "Same cities: actual greedy costs by start").x_label("starting city ID").y_label("rounded tour cost").save_svg("build/nearest_tsp_starts.svg");
}
```
