> Capture contract, conclusion and references

The optional trace owns the original matrix/start, result route, actual eligible
trials/choices and final edge sums. Its quota counts one start unit, input rows
and weights, route IDs and individual trial/choice/edge records. Default1000000,
zero disables; actual admission failure returns no partial result. Ordinary
driver storage remains separate from captured input/history.

The invariant explains feasibility and the records explain each greedy choice.
The retained three-city cost4, geometric comparison and directed trap separate
valid routes, small bounded optima and absent general guarantees. Start sweeps
can improve a chosen finite result without certifying a global optimum for
arbitrary large inputs.

[Rosenkrantz, Stearns and Lewis, An analysis of several heuristics for the traveling salesman problem (1977)|https://doi.org/10.1137/0206041]
studies nearest-neighbor heuristics and their problem-dependent guarantees. This
report explicitly uses the native complete directed-matrix contract and its
bounded enumeration reference.

